insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '76756d96-98d7-40ec-a784-3b9de38800da'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d1ens3title$Immersion · The Asura and the Hara Centre$d1ens3title$,
  $d1ens3json$["In this festival an image of the deity is made, and the image is immersed. The tradition is still kept. Only last month the Ganesh festival was celebrated, and Ganesh too was immersed. Creation, sustenance, and dissolution — srishti, sthiti, and laya — belong together. Whatever has been created is gathered back. It has to return to laya. We have come in the same way, into this round of form, and we too will be dissolved.","Once, in a Shiva temple, the same law stood in a sculpture. Dr Newton watches when he enters a temple. He sits, and he tries to read the inner sense of the architecture and the images. In meditation, something of the power of the place can be known by experience. There an asura had been set. He had a name; the name is not the point. His nature was to eat whatever appeared. He consumed everything he saw. When nothing else remained, he began to eat himself. The legs went, the hands, the trunk, up to the neck. Only the bare head was left. If he ate that too, he would no longer be there. Then awareness came. If I eat this, I am finished. I am dissolving. I will not remain.","So the science of the Self, this knowledge, this path of meditation, is a finishing of ourselves. We came forth in creation. We have to be brought to an end: immersion again, and a withdrawal. As one moves toward moksha, liberation, one dissolves. What dissolves is the aham that lives here, the sense of I.","Where is aham? This is the voice that says I, I am here, my name, my town, this is mine. It sits in the belly. That is why Rama could send arrow after arrow at the head, and strike the heads away, and still that many-headed asura, Ravana, did not die. Rama could not end him by the head. He struck the belly. The seat of it is the Manipura chakra, the centre at the navel. That is the will. It is also called the Hara centre. However often the head is cut, the one in the belly remains.","Spirituality is the ending of that aham, the complete dissolving of our I. Otherwise the I and the mine block the way. For any progress in meditation, the one obstruction is this limited I. The definition we live inside is very small. Our truth is the unlimited I, spread through everything. To come to know that, Haraki is required. Haraki means the will that sits in the Hara centre: our private sankalpa, I must do this, I must do that. Haraki is the finishing of ourselves. That is the real immersion.","A festival has a structure for this reason. Behind the customs set down by the ancestors, the yogis and the rishis, yogic secrets have been spoken. The immersion at the end is one of those secrets, acted out where everyone can see it."]$d1ens3json$::jsonb,
