insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '8b5ff2ba-1b4f-4f0f-a919-adc58f3fde4d'::uuid,
  d.id,
  4,
  $d1ens4title$Nights for the Sadhaka · One Shakti, Many Names$d1ens4title$,
  $d1ens4json$["Why are these called Navaratri, nine nights? Could the worship not be done in the morning? This too has an inner meaning. Bathukamma means living. It means life, and the celebrating of this life. When a word is born, a secret stands behind it. In these days the nine Durgas are worshipped, or a fresh decoration is made each day, with flowers and with different colours, and on the last day the whole is dissolved. Every festival has an outline because the outline matches creation, sustenance, and dissolution.","Whatever is in the brahmanda, the great cosmos, is in the pindanda, the small body. It happens in the subtle, in the causal world, in the great causal, and in the gross world as well. As above, so below. What has taken place in the higher worlds takes place here, and in us.","Night, in ordinary speech, is a symbol of tamoguna, the quality of heaviness. For yogis, for those who meditate, night means that time for sadhana has been given. It is a most sacred hour. No one disturbs it. Everyone else is asleep. The sadhaka wakes at three and sits. The morning is for work and for the matters of our several worlds. The night is for practice.","This Devi Navaratri belongs to Devi Shakti. Parameshwari is the root of everything, the support of this Prakriti and of whatever has been created. That support is worshipped as Durga Mata. The same Shakti is Lalita Mata. The same Shakti is the Mother of the world. In every village the gramadevata, the village goddess, is Parameshwari too. One power, many names. The confusion — how many powers, how many names — comes because the one is seen in many forms. Different regions gave different names. The root has to be held.","We call these days Sri Devi Navaratri. Sri means Prakriti shining. Devi means that which shines. All of Prakriti stands in the radiance of this Shakti. We do not see it, because Prakriti meets the eyes as a feast for the eyes. In every beauty we are absorbed. Behind the beauty a Shakti is standing. The whole moving and unmoving creation is made by this divine power. Without Shakti there is no creation. On that support this life itself is she. We are only calling her by separate names. The name Durga has to be opened."]$d1ens4json$::jsonb,
