insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '8e7699fe-4547-4921-a9ec-490428675911'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d1ens6title$Nine Enclosures · Gunas and the Nirguna$d1ens6title$,
  $d1ens6json$["She dwells in the Sri Chakra, and that Sri Chakra is here itself. Belonging to it are the navavaranas. Across these nine days the one who practises crosses them. The names are Sanskrit, and each name says what that enclosure does.","The first is Trailokya Mohana, the enclosure that enchants the three worlds. She is the dweller in the Sri Chakra: Sri Mata, the auspicious Mother; Sri Maharajni, the great queen; Srimat Simhasaneshwari, sovereign upon the lion throne. Lalita, Durga, and the Mother of the world are she. Sarvasha Paripuraka fulfils every hope. We have desires and expectations; if what we dream is to come true, this Shakti is required, and devotion with it. Sarva Sankshobhana stirs everything. Sarva Saubhagyadayaka grants good fortune. Sarvartha Sadhaka accomplishes every aim. Sarva Rakshakara extends every protection. Sarva Rogahara lessens our diseases and removes them. Sarva Siddhiprada grants the siddhis, the accomplished powers. Sarvanandamaya gives paramananda, the supreme bliss. Nine months, nine nights, nine enclosures. The sadhaka is not collecting nine goddesses. The sadhaka is being led, enclosure by enclosure, toward the one Shakti.","The whole of creation also stands in the trigunas, the three qualities, in every combination. And we live in three states: jagrat, waking; svapna, dream; sushupti, deep sleep. Three times three. Each state can be tamas, rajas, or sattva, according to our condition. Tamas is the lowest vibration. Rajas is a middle condition. Sattva is a finer one. The most excellent lies beyond them. Only when the gunas are crossed does nirguna arrive, the state without qualities. That is the true nature of the Atman. No quality remains. It is Parabrahman, Parameshwari, the supreme Parameshwara. Here on earth, everything still stands with some quality.","Night is a symbol of tamas, and with night come sleep and laziness. For the sadhaka that same hour is the hour of practice, about three hours at ease, watching inhalation and exhalation. The breath itself is prana. When people say the prana has gone, they mean the breath has stopped. As one expression, this prana-shakti carries out many functions and drives the body. The Shakti on whose support creation runs has to be known, experienced, become. We become Durga Mata herself through meditation. These nine days are for that daily practice."]$d1ens6json$::jsonb,
