insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '9cbe170f-266d-4175-a5a6-30d4cf31ef71'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d2ens3title$Three Mothers · Meditation, Service, Knowledge$d2ens3title$,
  $d2ens3json$["In these days of Devi Navaratri we worship her in many forms, and we call her by many names. We give forms to Para Shakti. When a form is given, a saguna aspect, a form with qualities, we work with that form each day. In some regions this Devi Shakti is worshipped chiefly in three forms: as Mother Saraswati, as Mother Lakshmi, and as Mother Durga, who is also Mother Kali.","Certain bija aksharas, seed syllables, have been spoken for them: Aim, Kreem, and Kleem. These seed syllables belong to the divine powers. Through vibrations of sound, the vibration of Devi Shakti was recognised, and those energies were identified by sound.","Aim is Mother Saraswati: vidya, learning, and knowledge. Kreem is aishwarya, prosperity, Mother Lakshmi. Kleem is Mother Kali, and Kali is Durga, and Durga is meditation. Mother Lakshmi is service. Mother Saraswati is atma jnana, knowledge of the Self. These three points have to be held. Three powers: this is the sadhana of the tri-shakti, the three powers. The whole of Navaratri is only these three — meditation, service, and Self-knowledge, which is brahma jnana, knowledge of the absolute. When these three powers are understood well, an activation happens. For anyone still stuck on the spiritual path and wishing to move to the next level, this sadhana is of great use.","So through these nine days let us remain in a feeling of offering. We call it Devi Samarpan, the offering of oneself to the Mother. In that offering we have to stay connected with ourselves in every matter. In everyone, the Mother is to be seen. Only Devi is to be seen. Even when we look at ourselves in a mirror, the darshan we take should be the darshan of Devi alone.","We are to say Amma, Amma. Whomever we meet: Amma, please tell me. Even when the person met is a man: please tell me, Amma. Only the Mother is to be seen there. Everyone is the Mother. Every person you meet, and every person you speak with, is Amma, the very form of the Mother. Para Shakti herself has manifested here in this way. This whole creation is she.","We began by naming Shakti and Chaitanya. Chaitanya means Shiva. If Shakti is absent, that Shiva too remains Shiva only in name. What would he do? He can do nothing. Shakti comes first. Shakti is the one that matters. Without Shakti, Shiva himself becomes a shava, a corpse. Without her there is nothing. This is why our texts have given a form, a beautiful picture in symbols. When she came as Mahishasura Mardini, the slayer of the buffalo demon, she took an incarnation and assumed a form, and she fought with a terrible force. She destroyed them all. She destroyed every asura, and such was the energy that she was on the point of destroying the gods as well. Shiva then had to go and stop her.","He lay down beneath her. She placed one foot upon him, and she placed that foot upon his heart. At that touch she came into a state of awareness. This is Chaitanya. This is Shiva.","That is why, through these nine days, the way we sit is particular. Ordinarily we sit cross-legged. Now the left foot should rest above, upon the right foot. Even on a chair, the left leg is placed over the right. Let us practise it as a symbol. The hands are usually kept with the right hand above and the left hand within. Instead of that, the left hand should lie above, covering the right. We are changing, a little, the way we sit, and we are doing it with awareness.","From childhood our parents nourished us and brought us up. We spent a great deal of time with them. We passed many hours in their company, and we felt their presence. We feel it even now. In memory, the sweet moments spent with one's parents never scatter. They remain, laid down within. They can be felt. Even if those parents have already left the body and gone on to the higher worlds, their presence can still be felt, because a connection was formed. By living close to them, the sense of their presence — you are here, you are with me — became printed in us.","That same presence, through these nine days of Devi Navaratri, is now to be felt as the presence of Para Shakti, of Adi Shakti. Presence is what matters. Feel her presence. She, that Shakti, is our mother. Chaitanya is our father. For this physical body there is a mother, and for this physical body there is a father. What is being spoken of now is the spiritual sense of it. Our mother is Shakti.","Through these nine days let us feel the presence of Devi Shakti, of Devi Mata. If the presence is to be felt, the experience has to come. The feeling has to be gained. A form is not required for that. She keeps giving darshan in many forms. The nirguna, that which is without qualities, becoming saguna, with qualities — Adi Shakti, Para Shakti, who has neither shape nor name, taking a form upon herself — many yogis and many rishis have seen how she then appears.","The pictures we see, and what stands in the temples, include her form as Mahishasura Mardini. Why did that particular darshan happen? So many people of meditation, seated in the meditative state, wished to behold the form of Para Shakti. What is nirguna and nirakara, without form, gives the eyes nothing to rest on. We cannot find a relation with it. When a name and a form arise, that is the darshan which came to them. On these days of Devi Navaratri, though, what matters is to feel the presence. Presence does not ask for a form, and it does not ask for a name. It is Shakti. By whatever name you call, hold her as Amma. That is enough. By whatever name she is called, she comes in that name. Child, Mother is coming. She gathers everyone close. She draws them near.","So the points to be practised through these nine days are these. First, the feeling of presence. Devi Mata is right here. She is within us. She is not someone else, and we are not someone else. We become her. We are not apart from that Shakti. That Shakti is what we are. At the beginning, though, we see her as apart, and if the feeling is to arise the presence has to be felt. Second, the Lakshmi mudra, which is the way of sitting. The left foot rests on the right foot. The left hand rests above the right hand. Greater place is given to the feminine principle.","These nine days are offered wholly to the Mother, wholly to Mata Shakti, in surrender. Without Shakti, Shiva too is only a corpse."]$d2ens3json$::jsonb,
