insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '1412a2e2-3399-4869-aba9-5cf3741a1919'::uuid,
  d.id,
  5,
  $d2ens5title$From the Soul · Red, the Dynamic Colour$d2ens5title$,
  $d2ens5json$["Celebration is a different matter from going out to drink and to dance. When many people hear the word festival, they turn to drink, or to something of that kind. A name has been given to that, and the name is enjoyment. Celebration comes from the soul. It is what arises from our Shakti, from atma chaitanya, the consciousness of the Self. To live each moment as a wonder, in a great joy — that is celebration. Nothing further has to be arranged in order to celebrate. Devi Navaratri itself means celebration for us, because we are joined to our own Shakti.","We are connected with that divine power, our Devi Mata, Kundalini Mata, the Mother as the Shakti within. We are near her. This nearness itself is the great celebration, a festival full of spirit. That is why the festival carries the name Dasara. Reverse Dasara, and the name becomes Sharada, to be lived with a light and playful gladness. Dasara means celebration.","Among the points of practice is Devi Samarpan: a complete offering to that Mother, Jagadamba, Mother of the world. Feel her presence completely. Shakti is that presence. Mother means Devi Mata, means Jagadamba, means the one whose very form is Shakti. Within us she shines as Adi Shakti, as the root power, as Kundalini. We have to surrender to that Mother. The feeling of offering has to remain.","There are two mudras here, the Vishnu mudra and the Lakshmi mudra. The Lakshmi mudra is the way you sit, and the way you place the body. The left foot, or the left hand, stays above. Through these nine days we remain inward, rather than pouring ourselves outward. Let us set aside, for these nine days, the small pleasures and the small enjoyments. After the nine days they may be taken up again. These nine days are dedicated to realising our own energy, and to entering that experience. Let us give the nine days to that.","In our India this festival is also kept through signs. Red clothes are worn. Dr. Newton himself had dressed in red, a red border, white together with red. In the food as well, let there be what is red. Tomatoes are red. Among the capsicums there is a green one and a red one. Among the vegetables, some are red and some are yellow. This is the dynamic energy of that Shakti. When Adi Shakti, in her saguna form, manifests in the physical and expresses herself physically, the colour of that dynamic energy is red. That is why kumkum, the vermilion, is so deeply red. All of this has been set in place among us as a symbol, so that we may stay near her. If she is to be known, and if there is to be surrender to that Mother, then red clothing and red-coloured food — red, or white and red. Red is a very dynamic colour. These are signs.","Behind every story we read, a symbol is waiting. In that symbolic form our rishis and maharshis composed the Devi Bhagavatam and the puranas. Many inner meanings are held there. A great knowledge was placed inside those stories. All of it was set down for sadhana. These festivals exist for sadhana. One part is celebration, the keeping of the festival. The other part is coming to know, and the practice itself.","The breath, too, ordinarily moves. It happens of itself. After we rise in the morning, Devi Samarpan is an offering, made in a symbolic form, to the Shakti which conducts this whole creation — the Shakti on whose support this entire brahmanda goes on."]$d2ens5json$::jsonb,
