insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '70386cc5-d73b-4348-aa9f-34db84203806'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d2ens6title$Chandra Nadi · Bowing the Head$d2ens6title$,
  $d2ens6json$["That breath keeps changing through the two nostrils. Moment by moment it shifts, left and right, right and left. In everyone it changes about every hour and a half, or every two hours. After we have sat in meditation, the two become equal. When they are equal, the energy flows upward through the sushumna, the central channel. The direction changes.","When you are active and dynamic, the right side is the one that runs. That is the masculine, the movement toward the outside, the greater activity. You may be doing some work, or you may be thinking a great deal, and thinking too is a work. Whenever you are somewhat quiet, whenever you are going into yourself and withdrawing, the chandra nadi, the lunar channel, is the first to become active. The breath then moves through the left nostril.","This is the sign of Shakti. If the right side is taken as Chaitanya, the left side is Shakti. The meeting of the two travels inward and touches the ajna chakra, the centre between the eyebrows. It is the union of Shiva and this Shakti, the union of Shakti and Chaitanya, the union of the lunar channel and the surya nadi, the solar channel. That union expresses itself as the sushumna, and as the agni nadi, the channel of fire. So every day, after rising in the morning, a process is to be done. Through these nine days let us do it, and in the evening as well — once, and a second time. It is a way of reminding ourselves. Devi Samarpan: remaining in the feeling of offering toward Adi Shakti, toward Para Shakti.","In our culture, bowing is something learned in childhood from the elders. We offer namaskara at a person's feet. We bend the head. When we went near God, mother and father would say: bend the head, and offer your namaskara. Sometimes a sashtanga pranam is offered, the homage of the whole body. This matters greatly. We bow to our parents as well. We salute their feet, bending the head.","A temple is built in the same knowledge. There are many crossings, and as one goes inward, toward the garbhagudi, the inner sanctum, the doorway grows small. Door by door, the openings become smaller. The head has to bend. This head is ahamkara, the ego, and it can become a very large ego. We say that pride has risen into the head. We say it has gone to his head. Money comes, power comes, status comes, a position comes, and for many people all of it goes straight into the head. The ego arrives.","The head has to bow. To Devi Mata, to this Shakti, the head has to bow. Upon this Shakti the whole creation rests. Every single thing happens only through Shakti. Bow the head. These things were taught to us, toward our parents, when we were small. If they were not learned then, they can be learned now. Bow at the feet of your parents. Whether they are present or absent, even if they live only in the heart, bow to the feet of our mother and our father. Had they not been, we would not have come here at all. To Shakti and to Chaitanya also — mother and father of the soul that has come onto this earthly sphere — we offer namaskara. Through these nine days we bow to that Mother. In Devi Samarpan the head bends. Let us learn the process."]$d2ens6json$::jsonb,
  5
