insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'b7778680-d544-4e84-afec-4c5b78ec484e'::uuid,
  d.id,
  7,
  $d2ens7title$Devi Samarpan · Nine Breaths, Nine Times$d2ens7title$,
  $d2ens7json$["Sit at ease. On a chair, sit as was shown. The left leg rests above the right leg. Seated on a chair, place the left leg over the right. In this sign we give the preference to the feminine principle, to Adi Shakti. In the same manner that Mother Kali set her foot upon Shiva's chest, we tune ourselves to Shakti, and a connection forms.","The hand is held in Vishnu mudra. Close the index finger and the middle finger, and leave the thumb and the other fingers open. The mudra stands in the shape of the shankha, Vishnu's conch, and with it the right nostril is closed. The right nostril is the surya nadi, the solar channel. It is closed because the chandra nadi, the lunar channel, is Shakti. As a sign, and in order to tune to the feminine principle, this lunar channel is what first helps us become inward. Watch the placing of the hand before you begin.","Near the right nostril apply only a little pressure. Do not press hard. Without troubling the nose, close it gently and slowly. Then draw the breath in nine times, through the left nostril, one sip after another, step by step. Do not breathe out between them. The nine breaths are a filling. The breath is taken in until it is complete. If each sip is too large, the nine cannot be finished, because the lungs are already full. So draw slowly, a little at a time, nine times, until the breath is held inside, in kumbhaka, the inner retention.","Then both hands leave the Vishnu mudra and join in namaskara. Place the joined palms upon the head, because the head is about to bow. Those nine inhalations are still within. Now bend the head with the feeling of Devi Samarpan: I offer myself. The feeling is the whole of it. A merely mechanical bow is not the practice. With the sense that the offering to the Mother has been made, the head comes down, and as the head bows, the breath is released outward.","The inner hold cannot be kept for long. So, with the hands joined, bow slowly, as one bows at the feet, and let the breath go out while the head descends. If the earth can be touched, so much the better. Sitting cross-legged on the floor, let the namaskara meet the ground, and let the head meet the ground if it will. The feeling of the touch is Devi Samarpan. This Shakti of the Mother of the world, this Para Shakti who has made the whole creation, this Para Shakti who is within me — I surrender. I offer myself. If the head cannot reach the earth, let the hands touch the earth at least. The head itself must bend. In that bowing the ego is laid down completely. Our ego is our chief enemy.","As we have bowed to mother and to father, we bow to this Mother also: Jagadamba, Mother Durga, Mother Kali in her many forms, Mother Lakshmi, Mother Saraswati. When she came as Mahishasura Mardini, all the gods came and gave their powers into her. All of them are in her. The shakti of every god joined wholly into her. Mother Lakshmi is in her. Mother Saraswati is in her. Mother Durga is in her. All are she — Adi Shakti, in whom every power is gathered. She became the slayer of Mahishasura. On one of the coming days that inner meaning will be decoded. Today the process is what we learn.","When the breath has gone fully out, remain there. That is bahir kumbhaka, the outer retention. Do not draw the breath in again yet. Stay bowed. For how long? For a count of nine. Or, if you wish, for nine repetitions of Shreem. Shreem, the seed syllable Shrim, belongs to Devi Mata and is for joining with her. At the para level, the subtle level of sound, it is not pronounced aloud outside. It repeats within the consciousness. Nine times Shreem, or nine quiet counts. In that surrender, with the head bowed, remain. Sharanu, sharanu, sharanu: I take refuge. I surrender to that Shakti. Today I surrender to Shakti. After the nine, return slowly, and raise the head again.","This is one round, and the round is done nine times. Done with care, the whole of it takes about three minutes. It is to be learned today and practised each day. This is Devi Samarpan.","He guided the gathering through the rounds, and asked that each round be done consciously, with the feeling kept awake. On the later rounds the head was to touch the earth where it could, the breath was to be emptied completely, and in that emptiness Shreem was thought inside, never thrown outward. Along with the bowing he asked that the ego be offered, and everything with it. Let every single thing be placed at her feet. Surrender to Shakti. Whatever problems are here, these hard and difficult troubles, however many they are, surrender them. There are many sankalpas, many resolves. That state has to be reached, and her power is what is needed. Take refuge.","In one round the offering was of all the problems. In another it was of the concerns and the worries as well: surrender them to Adi Shakti, to Para Shakti, the power that has made this whole creation, and remain in a complete feeling of refuge while Shreem sounds nine times within, as a vibration of sound. Then the head comes slowly upright, back to its ordinary place. Between rounds, a little rest is allowed. The breath is never forced.","The last round he left to each person alone, with no further instruction, so that what had been learned could be done in one's own feeling of surrender.","There are many things we are not able to solve. And our own power, however much sadhana we have done and however much energy we have gathered within, remains a limited power. If we can trust this Shakti completely, and surrender, then that force — the divine force — is what solves them. She makes us able to cross them, whatever the obstacle may be. She is our mother. After rising in the morning, in a meditative state, let us do this offering, and let us remain in a complete surrender to Shakti."]$d2ens7json$::jsonb,
