insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '55860cdd-fb1c-4aca-a507-cc445e574ed7'::uuid,
  d.id,
  11,
  $d2ens11title$Naresh · The Eighteen Seats of Shakti$d2ens11title$,
  $d2ens11json$["Naresh offered his namaskara. He has been meditating for seven years, though without a steady regularity. For one month now he has been attending Jnanodayam. He has listened to the twelve classes of Shwasa Mahavidya, the great teaching of the breath. The answer to every question, he said, is here itself, inside Jnanodayam. And today, through this class, he had been able to learn how pranama, the reverent bow, is offered to Jagadamba, to Shakti. Then he asked that the Ashtadasha Shakti Peethas, the eighteen seats of Shakti, might be explained.","Every Shakti Peetha, Dr. Newton said, is a continuity. Look at the way these seats are built. They are raised with enormous weight, with many tons of stone. In a Shakti Peetha there is a temple, and it is very ancient. Why should it be so? For the sake of continuity. There the Shakti is held in an unbroken stream. A Jyotirlinga, a linga of light, or any one of the eighteen Shakti Peethas: always there is a parampara, a succession reaching back a very long way. That great power, present from so long ago, keeps being built up. The sadhakas who go there and sit in meditation are deeply fulfilled, and the energy of the place is put to use.","The eighteen seats would be spoken of in the classes still to come. Seven days of this Navaratri remain. They can be taken up in detail, and if they are taken up properly the telling occupies more than an hour. It is, for him, a deeply interesting subject. These peethas are power spots. He and those who travel with him are wanderers upon them. They go, they practise, they sit in meditation, in order to feel that energy. The account of them would certainly be given. He thanked Naresh for the sharing."]$d2ens11json$::jsonb,
  3
