insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '3ad0ab50-99de-4a72-ab20-7b7dd6a60682'::uuid,
  d.id,
  2,
  $d3ens2title$Sura and Asura · Svara and Apasvara$d3ens2title$,
  $d3ens2json$["Who are the suras, and who are the asuras? Every festival has its distinction, and a puranic story. Those stories are written largely about asuras, and still more about rakshasas. Asura means rakshasa. Sura means the devata. Mahishasura is told at length in the Devi Bhagavatam, the Markandeya Purana, the Vamana Purana, and two or three other books. Films work the same way. The stronger the villain, the higher the hero is lifted. The contrast is enormous. Many rakshasas are heard of for that reason.","Before those two words, svara, and its opposite, apasvara, the missed note. In speech a mistake slips in. A meeting is going on, the microphone is in the hand, and what should not be said in that meeting gets said. Only what belongs to the context is to be spoken. When speech leaves rhythm and connection, apasvara has happened. A musician treats the note with that seriousness. Those who love music hear it at once: the svara is beautiful, or a phrase should have fallen higher, or lower, or the sequence was missed. Both are in us.","When life runs in rhythm, that is svara. When it slips, that is apasvara. The heart beats in a rhythm every day, whether we notice or not. Arrhythmia is the loss of it: too fast, a sudden double beat, then too slow, erratically, as it pleases. Breath too goes on in an order. This also is svara, which is why it is named Chandra-svara, the lunar channel, Surya-svara, the solar channel, and Sushumna-svara, the central channel. Breath becomes speech. Breath comes out as the word. It makes every action happen, and it changes into prana, the life-power, working in the body, the mind, and at the subtle level. If the breathing itself is off the note, the problems are all there.","Suras are those who remain in rhythm, in connection: body, mind, heart, the Self, the full Self, the supreme Self, the Self of all, in one link. Asuras are those from whom the link has gone. Then one thing is thought, another done, a third spoken. The body wants sleep. The mind is elsewhere. The wish inside is something else again. The mouth is making large promises: I meditate so much, I will change my whole life. The body is heavy with tamas, the mind restless with rajas, and word, deed, and thought do not agree.","Live inside the delusion of the body — I am the body — and the rhythm has already gone. What remains is laziness, fatigue, and the postponement of everything. What is to be done today: tomorrow, later, next month. These are marks by which the asura can be recognized."]$d3ens2json$::jsonb,
  4
