insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '0e6153dc-9e44-45cc-a351-6031fabbd323'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d3ens3title$Three Gunas · The Crown in the Mind$d3ens3title$,
  $d3ens3json$["Ravana was an asura. He was born a Brahmin, in a Brahmin line, and no one is a Brahmin by birth. He became a rakshasa all the same. He plays the veena beautifully, he meditates, he keeps up his recitation, and the asura-nature remains. The Brahmin is the one in whom knowledge of the Self has come, knowledge of Brahman. A bodily inheritance does pass down — a vegetarian way, certain rules, so that the genes express themselves well — but that is not Brahminhood. In terms of the Self, everyone gathered here is a meditator.","Mahishasura meditates too. Many rakshasas do tapas, and the question is why. For powers. For a life without death. So that no one can touch them, so that they alone may rule and command. There is no alignment. Desire follows the guna. Tamas desires one way, rajas another. Sattva is what we mean by a good person: the sattvic wish the good of all. The purely sattvic, and those beyond the gunas, wish differently again. Along with me, everyone. The joy I am receiving, everyone should receive. My share of the help; I too will make the effort. The support of the world.","He called those who have crossed the three gunas the amaras, the deathless. The Self in me is the Self in everyone. The supreme Self is everything: sarvam khalvidam Brahma, all this is indeed Brahman. They offer only for the welfare of the world. Service without a break, practice without a break, and the prayer sarve janah sukhino bhavantu, may all people be happy. A life ruled by tamas is gripped by two things, ego and the sense of mine. From that ego, often unnoticed, everything else is born.","A wish is not a fault. Without desire there is no creation. Hope is not a fault. Atyasha, the excess, is what makes us asuras. Wishing for wealth is not wrong. Wishing to be richer than everyone is where the trouble starts. The asura has slid from alignment into misalignment. A proportion is meant to be kept. Tamas is in sadhakas too, a small share, in the meditator perhaps one percent. Some rajas is required. With none, a person would not step out of the house; there would be no dynamism. Perhaps eight or nine percent. In meditators the greater share is sattva, the heart open, the person kind. The world, which has not practised, sees this and says: good people. Three proportions remain in everyone. In the asura, tamas and rajas are simply heavier. What is needed is symmetry.","Where tamas is more, violence is done, and laziness sets in. Things are thought and not done, and what is done asks for praise without a pause. Praise becomes an addiction. Where rajas is intense, fame is hunted. If it comes, let it come. The crawling for it, the willingness to do anything for name and position, is the trouble.","A king once went to a guru, wanting knowledge of the Self. He went as kings go: crown, golden armour, diamonds, vaidurya, every jewel. Take off what is weighing on you, the guru said. The king took off a crown of three or four kilos, and the armour, and called it easy. This is not what I meant, the guru said. A crown is set in the mind: I am a king, a very great man. Remove that. The armours that matter are worn inside. Ornaments, prestige — leave them, and then meditation settles, and the mind becomes empty. The outer crown can come off while the inner one has not moved. People hold a new resolve for forty days, ten days of not being angry, and then the anger returns, because the mind never let go. Rajas at that pitch is the same asura-nature. In the puranic stories a war between suras and asuras is always being staged. Every story is the same one story."]$d3ens3json$::jsonb,
