insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '1eea8622-fe27-4a46-a252-4e923c4da4ef'::uuid,
  d.id,
  4,
  $d3ens4title$One Story · The Battle Inside$d3ens4title$,
  $d3ens4json$["The lesson is one. A battle, and the two sides are not somewhere else. They are in us. Enemies are not outside. Rakshasas are not outside. Mahishasura is within us, and so are Shumbha and Nishumbha, Rambha and Karambha, and the many other names the books give. Another way of defining them is by quality. Bad qualities are called rakshasa qualities. They do what ought not to be done. Harm is adharma, at every hour and in every place. True Sanatana Dharma is ahimsa: to live without doing violence. The stories say that when munis and rishis sit in tapas, hot oil is poured on them and they are shaken out of meditation. That is the violent tendency. So is saying what should not be said, saying whatever comes to the mouth, having no right speech, no right conduct, no right way of seeing or of thinking. These express themselves as violence.","After his birth Mahishasura also did tapas. Rakshasas meditate well. Ego and the sense of mine remain, at an intense degree. Ravana crossed a boundary. In a disguise he took Sita to Lanka, without her consent, by force. He did violence to a woman, hurt her, and separated husband and wife. Rama grieved, longed for her, and suffered the separation. Sita thinks when she will see him. Mahishasura, Bhandasura, Vritrasura: the names are many, and all of them arise from the three gunas alone, in differing proportions, combinations, and permutations. Each villain has a character of his own because the ratio is his own.","The nine nights were set apart for sadhana, parva days, favourable to practice, and they are to be used. Inner change asks a great deal of practice. Used well, these days open a power already shining over the whole earth. On the first day the three powers were spoken of. Many join; principally there are three, which is why the festival speaks of three mothers. Durga, who is the avatara of Kali; then Lakshmi; Mahadurga, Mahalakshmi, Mahasaraswati. The first three days belong to Mahadurga, the power that carries a person across whatever is hard, whatever obstructs, whatever interrupts. In plain words, blocks. They are internal. Outside, they are not found. Whatever happens, happens within. These first three days, that is what is to be cleared first."]$d3ens4json$::jsonb,
  3
