insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'e193d3cb-b1d5-4c69-a29e-796bed6d0726'::uuid,
  d.id,
  2,
  $d4ens2title$The Fourth Morning · Cause, Symbol, and a New Birth$d4ens2title$,
  $d4ens2json$["Jnanodayam. A good morning, dear friends. Devi Navaratri is being celebrated from inside a great state that can be felt. This fourth day, too, belongs to Jagannmata, the Mother of the world, and to Parashakti, the supreme power, in her many forms and aspects. For a reason, she created herself. Every happening is an effect of a cause. Where there is no cause, there is no effect. Our birth has a cause. So has every creation.","From the first day, the point has been that every story in our scriptures carries a hidden meaning, a knowledge placed beneath the surface. Every festival in India, and every divine power, was given certain days by the maharshis. Devi Navaratri is of that kind. The puranic stories were told in signs. The more clearly they are understood, the more of that knowledge can be taken. They are the secrets and the skill of sadhana. Heard as bare stories, they yield nothing. A festival can feel joyful, and the moment it is over we become ordinary again.","Of Devi Navaratri, this was said at the start. In nine months a new birth is taken from inside the mother. These nine days belong to that same mystery, turned toward an energy and a dimension: Adishakti, Parashakti, Sridevi. We, too, are taking birth newly inside that dimension. The narrative we enter today has a beginning of its own. Mahishasura came into being. He was born. Behind that birth there is another story. There is a background. That background is what this morning is for."]$d4ens2json$::jsonb,
