insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '6a255833-9c90-4b09-a996-e374b4850c7e'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d4ens3title$Rambha and Karambha · Body and Endless Wanting$d4ens3title$,
  $d4ens3json$["The story opens with Rambha and Karambha. At the start they are asuras. Their father is the danava Dhanu, and in the symbol he is the first ancestor of the danavas. Hear Dhanu, and another word stands beside it: tanu, the body. The name is body-identification, the delusion of the body, a tamasic tendency, the feeling that says I, this body alone. Only a physical identity. That is Dhanu, and that is tanu. To Dhanu two sons were born: Rambha and Karambha.","The names themselves are striking. In these stories, pairs of names are chosen so that duality can be recognized. Shumbha and Nishumbha are used in that way. Rambha and Karambha belong to the same kind of naming. When the two sons had grown a little, they undertook tapas. Rambha meditated. Karambha meditated. They were asuras, and they had set a purpose. They had set a goal. The goal was putra labha, the gaining of a son. Vedavyasa composed the episode in this shape because it is the shape of an ordinary human life.","After youth arrives, the wish appears. A son is wanted. The wish is for a son, a putra, the gain of a son. Rambha and Karambha, the writing says, meditated with severity for many thousands of years. While they were doing so, certain things happened. Those things are the teaching of this day, because Rambha and Karambha are also aspects in us. We, too, sit for meditation.","In Sanskrit, Rambha is said to be that which sounds, a ghosa, a resounding. It is also that which delights and sports. And the word is taken here as sound, as proclamation, and as aihika vancha: desire aimed at this world. I want all of this. The world of objects is full of desires, of thirsts, of wants. The want has a familiar face. We already have a fridge at home, and in the mall a larger fridge appears. Technology changes, and the moment the new thing is seen, that too is wanted. A television is bought, and a newer one is already there. A phone is bought, and next year another model is released, and another thirst begins.","Wherever we go, the mind swells with it. This must be bought. That must be bought. We see one house, and then another, and we feel that the other house must be ours. Each thing, as it appears, feels necessary."]$d4ens3json$::jsonb,
