insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'c3137a9a-ab98-4d87-a40f-6353ba6c716d'::uuid,
  d.id,
  5,
  $d4ens5title$Indra the Old Man · The Mind That Interrupts$d4ens5title$,
  $d4ens5json$["While Rambha and Karambha are meditating, jealousy rises in Indra. He becomes afraid that the place of Indra will be lost, that they will strike it down. We hear this in our films and in our stories, and it is the same here. Rambha is doing so much meditation, and doing it so severely. They will obtain boons. They will come against Indra's world and do as they please. My position will be lost. With that fear he comes, and he comes in the form of an old man.","Rambha is in the pond. The old man, it is said, caught him by the leg. He pulls, and he keeps pulling. This, too, is a symbol, and Vedavyasa's metaphor is a great one. What does Indra stand for? Among the organs, the chief organ is not only the five organs of knowledge. It is the mind, itself an organ. That Indra is this mind. Indra is the sign of the mind. Whoever sits for meditation, this mind is the first obstruction. The mind itself stands in the way. It gives birth to all the thoughts.","Our tendencies arrive, and they arrive from this mind. This is why the mind has to be made empty. The mind has to be cleared. The state without thought has to be reached first. Attention is placed on the breath, and placed there again, so that the movements of the chitta, the mind-stuff, may be stilled. The effort is there. And the Indra called mind puts on the form of an old man. Rambha is in the middle of his practice. The old man seizes him, pulls him, and in the end kills him. Rambha vacates the body. So the story says.","This is the condition of Rambha. In a form of maya, the Indra who is the mind will not let us practise. How he does it is what the story, in its symbol, has told."]$d4ens5json$::jsonb,
  3
from public.days d
