insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '8c792526-5b85-440b-aa40-84026431bf2c'::uuid,
  d.id,
  7,
  $d4ens7title$Two Desires · The Inner Teacher and the Balance$d4ens7title$,
  $d4ens7json$["That birth then stays an incomplete life. It has to be repeated. Whatever we meant to do, the clarity of it becomes known in meditation. This is why the practice is done. First we have to know who we are. Second, we have to know what we are to do. We speak of a purpose of life. What is the aim of this life? That has to be known.","Rambha remains inside his maya, the wants of this world. Karambha's wish is liberation, though asura qualities remain in him, as tamas and rajas remain in everyone. He goes on. When the mind had been dissolved, Agni appeared, and the tattva of Paramatman was known: subtler than the smallest particle, and vast through many universes. That is the tattva of the Atman. From it his prabodha came, the inner teaching. Step out of the field of feeling and into the field of the Atman, and instruction comes from there. The wish to hear it only from outside falls away. We hear it within. The Guru is inside us, a great Guru. Paramatman waits within as this jivatma. The God who lives inside is waiting for the moment we connect. That teaching is atma-prabodha. The Atman, Paramatman, makes many things known.","The wish for a son means the wish to cross birth and death. That is the great goal. Through moksha-jnana those cycles stop. In school the same class is failed, and the same lesson studied, again and again. He recited Bhaja Govindam: punarapi jananam punarapi maranam, punarapi janani jathare shayanam; iha samsare bahu dustare, kripaya pare pahi murare. Again birth, again death, again lying in a mother's womb. This samsara is hard to cross. By your grace, O Murari, protect me. Bhaja Govindam. We keep coming. Many births go to waste, and this is not even realized.","Here, while a child is still unborn, once conception has happened, the first thought of the parents is the school fee, paid in advance. These days they decide the school, an international school, and they programme a small child. They write the child's life-script beforehand. He must become a doctor. The two parents argue. No: this child must become a scientist. She says, a doctor. From childhood the programming is finished.","After the birth there is confusion. The child does not know what to do. The parents carry their own projects. She could not become a doctor, so my son must become a doctor, my daughter must, she must do all of this. We are born for an agenda that belongs to the one who is born. There is a soul's agenda. There is a soul's plan.","We are not here for survival alone. Survival is important. It is very important, certainly, on this physical plane. The dharma of a profession is done. We remain in that profession. We should not become occupied by that alone. Be as Lahiri Mahasaya was. He did the dharma of his work. He kept a nine-to-five post, and all night he practised. He held the two in balance.","Rambha and Karambha are brothers, which means that within us one jivatma wishes to practise, and two desires are present. One desire says: I must also survive here. I must work. I need a salary. I must maintain a family. I need a family. I need comfort. I need enjoyment on the physical plane. That is the state of Rambha. Karambha says: I must attain moksha. I must attain that supreme bliss. I must experience what Sri Krishna, Paramatman, has said in the Bhagavad Gita. I must attain knowledge of the Self and knowledge of Brahman. That is the mental state of Karambha.","Inside the jiva itself, both are there. There are dualities. On one side, samsara. On the other, nirvana. In samsara we want every enjoyment. In nirvana we want the state without sorrow, the state of supreme bliss. We want that knowledge of liberation, asking when the sorrows will go.","The two sankalpas contradict each other. One leads toward materialism, the other toward the spiritual life. This duality is Rambha and Karambha, and the doubt is plain: samsara, or nirvana? Many leave the household life on the outside, and by force. I want nothing. I will take sannyasa. I will only meditate. Inside, it keeps pulling. This experience was never had. This was not done, and that was not done. All of it is incomplete. I left my family. I speak to no one. I have become solitary. In samsara the marks are zero, and then in nirvana we fall back down. Are we shining there? Are we progressing? We are at zero on this side because we ran away, and the score is in the negative. Run from samsara, and the problem returns. The sadhaka balances both. Both are important. Neither is to be abandoned."]$d4ens7json$::jsonb,
  7
