insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '4d1409fc-5fff-41d0-a6af-700c11b46baf'::uuid,
  d.id,
  10,
  $d4ens10title$The Dirty Cloth · Vasanas and the Mine Within$d4ens10title$,
  $d4ens10json$["A man went to a siddha, a perfected person. There was a dirty cloth. The master, in order to teach something, gave him that dirty cloth and told him to clean it. The story is a symbol. The man dipped the cloth in water and brought it back. The dirt was still there. A little of it had gone, and the cloth was otherwise the same. The master said: go, and rub it well with soap.","He went and rubbed it with soap. Two or three times, and the dirt was gone. He came back, and the smell had not gone. The smells were still there. Go again, and wash it thoroughly. He washed it as the master had said, and washed it well. Then the bad odor also left. This is a story in the form of a sign.","We too carry many samskaras and many vasanas, impressions held through many births. The asura qualities are themselves vasanas: tamas, and the ego that belongs to rajas. There is a great deal of dirt, and the angle from which we see is not yet right. The main point of the day is for practice. Whenever sorrow comes, recall it at once. Someone has scolded you, or spoken critically, or not spoken to you at all, and you are disappointed or upset. The cause of that sorrow is my avidya, my ignorance. Other people are not the cause. We ourselves are the cause of our sorrow, and we ourselves are the cause of our joy. Hold that one truth, and let the inner dirt go. There is, they say, a great mine of gold in us, a great treasure, and it is in everyone.","A man was living in poverty. Every day he had to labor hard. A siddha was there, under a tree, and the man would go. Once he offered his namaskara. Swami said: My dear man, do you not know? In the backyard of your own house, dig. Dig three feet down. A mine will be found there. It is in your house. The poor man did not believe it. What, in our house? It is an ordinary house. A master, a yogeshwara, says: it is in you. Everything is in you. A great tattva of the Atman is in you. Your Atman — everything will be found. The Chintamani, the wish-fulfilling gem, will be found. The Akshaya Patra, the vessel that is never exhausted, will be found. Kamadhenu, the wish-yielding cow, will be found. Whatever you think, that will come to pass. You are a very great one. So the master says. We do not believe. It does not seem possible to believe. He, too, did not believe. He lived on in that poverty, and one day he died.","Many people, already, knew the matter the Guru had spoken. Another man then bought that house and came to live in it. The Guru had said it. This man believed. He went and dug. As soon as he dug, a great mine of gold was found there. In us, too, there is a great mine. What the Gurus say is this same matter. In us there is what we call bad, and what we call good: rajas, tamas, and sattva along with them, and divine qualities as well. We are divine beings. We are Atmans. We are those who stand with the tattva of Paramatman. This has to be remembered. In Devi Navaratri, that state is what we are to attain.","An infinite energy is asleep in us, lying dormant. It is Jagannmata, Adishakti. If we practise, we become able to conquer those enemies, one by one. The mind, this chief enemy, and this maya as well, we conquer. We come to know that we ourselves are the cause of our joy."]$d4ens10json$::jsonb,
