insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '1db7de50-ed5f-498c-a7f1-6ea23e025406'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d5ens3title$Rambha and Karambha · Two Desires$d5ens3title$,
  $d5ens3json$["Behind the stories there is an inner meaning, a hidden meaning, and it has to be known. Yesterday we learned of Rambha and Karambha. Rambha stands for aihika vanchas — desires of this world. There are two directions of desire, iha and para. Iha means here, this earth-realm. Here certain wants arise: this is wanted, that is wanted. Para is the desire turned toward what is not visible. I want to see what these eyes cannot see. I want to hear what these ears cannot hear. Both desires live in a jiva. In the jiva, Rambha is there, and Karambha is there too.","Rambha makes the material desires of samsara arise. After this body is taken, they are born through the five senses and the mind. They are temporary, and still hope fastens on money: earn more, and for twenty-four hours there is no other sadhana. People meditate on money, or on a job, or on this want and that. That is Rambha. Even there a longing remains — I must know what has to be known, the unknown reality. This is not it; all this is maya — and awareness stays caught. Yesterday: Indra, and mind itself is Indra, came as an old woman, seized Rambha, and destroyed him. She keeps pulling us. Suppress those desires, press them down, and sometime they come out again.","That is why the sadhaka’s Karambha-state is different. The desires are still there, but he has done sadhana in the midst of the panchagnis, the five fires — meaning, amid the five organs of knowledge, and in nature. The banyan, in that telling, is nature: the Paramatma-principle present in nature. He has already started, in seed form, the sadhana of Sarvam khalvidam Brahma. I must attain the knowledge of moksha, enlightenment, the one knowledge after which no other knowledge is required. With that he began meditation.","Karambha’s sankalpa is intense: moksha, mukti, the crossing of birth and death. In yesterday’s story both did tapas for putra-labha, a son. That word, taken here, means the crossing of the cycle. Both live in us, and both are asuras. Asura-guna is the binding quality of nature, chiefly tamas. Tamoguna makes a prisoner of us. Rajoguna does the same: captivity to praise, fame, position, money, property, gold, and people — mine, I, mine — until ahankara and mamakara stay. Tamoguna is also total identification with the body, and laziness for sadhana. Other work does not bring sleep. Meditation does. Many fall asleep as soon as practice begins. And yet a divine radiance is in me.","On the first day someone asked about the eighteen shakti-pithas. All of them are in this body. In the commentary the Ajna chakra is the city of Kashi. Kashi means that which shines — our Ajna. Immerse there, as one immerses in the Ganga. The physical Ganga is not a mistake; those waters are sacred, because the commentary is representing, in symbol, what is within. The seventy-two thousand nadis are spoken of as seventy-two thousand rivers, and the rivers of India carry those names. All the jyotirlingas are inside. What is searched for outside has to be seen inside: darshan, knowledge, experience. Held only outside, it never leaves, and contentment never comes. Contentment comes when buddhi blossoms. What is asked is material contentment together with spiritual discontent."]$d5ens3json$::jsonb,
