insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'f72786b3-d936-4d16-aaff-fb2110fffaea'::uuid,
  d.id,
  5,
  $d5ens5title$Mahishasura · The Hidden Yoga$d5ens5title$,
  $d5ens5json$["Karambha offered his head to Agni and was told to ask a boon. He asked to cross birth and death. Outwardly the story says he asked for an unconquerable son of great strength. Misread, the effort looks like the ordinary wish for a child: as soon as people marry, when will the children come; a girl is born and there is disappointment; a boy is wanted, then another. It is symbolic. Every Puranic story in India is written that way. Karambha’s sadhana is deliverance from Punnama Naraka, the hell of dying without a son. The seed was planted, and the practice was intense.","Agni said: whoever appears first, whomever the mind first touches, through that one a son will come. He opened his eyes. A mahishi appeared, a she-buffalo. Mahi is the earth, this nature, and what is in nature is the Paramatma-principle, so Sarvam khalvidam Brahma appeared as the mahishi. He united with her and merged with her. The inner meaning is tadatmya, identity with that nature — a yoga secret in the language of sadhana.","She became pregnant: a knowledge ripening in him, a fruit coming ready. At that moment another buffalo, driven by lust, attacks and falls on her by force. Told as a violation, the subtle sense is an obstacle. Even in meditation a residue that had stayed inside comes out and is about to interrupt. Karambha fights, and Karambha is slain. The mahishi alone, the sadhana alone, is protected by the yakshas — the knowledge-power present invisibly, which keeps the practice distinct, subtle, and unperished.","When Karambha is placed on the pyre, Mahishasura is born from her womb: the passage from the Karambha-state to the Mahishasura-state. The sadhana was still a seed. It had not matured. The same story says Karambha came out of that pyre again as Raktabija. Without the keys, only the outer sense is taken. The firangis, the Westerners who made India a land of slaves, laugh at this culture and at this spiritual science. They say these people have this kind of encounter even with a beast. Vedavyasa did not write idly. He classified the Vedas and the Upanishads, and they are still within our reach. He is verily the form of Vishnu. Behind every story there is an inner meaning.","Mahishasura is an asura. Asura, asvara, nasvara: that which perishes and destroys, what is not good, what keeps destroying sadhana. Asuras disturb meditators, yogis, yajnas and homas — pouring blood from above, as the stories say. Whatever bad quality interrupts practice is asura-guna, and it is in us. Tamas and rajas are these. In us are Rambha, Karambha, and Mahishasura, and also Shumbha and Nishumbha, Vritrasura, Raktabija, and all the Ravanas. The deva-guna, the divinity, is in us as well, and the rakshasas destroy that. Sadhakas propitiate the Devi Shakti within and enter oneness with her. She slays them. These nine days eliminate, one by one, whatever rakshasa quality is left in a corner.","Once arisen, Mahishasura declared war on Indra, fought, and threw the gods into terror. They pleaded with the Trimurtis. Vishnu came on Garuda. Garuda was struck and fell; taking Vishnu, he fled. Garuda is the symbol of Veda-knowledge and of Upanishad-knowledge. Indirect knowledge, paroksha jnana, does not serve in sadhana. Mere theory — all the Vedas read — scores zero. Practice is required. Rituals and ceremonies are good, and nothing is wrong in doing them, but the inner meaning has to be known. Many get stuck there. That is why Garuda falls. Mahishasura defeats Indra and rules Indraloka.","From all the worlds they came to one congregation, Vishnu leading, and he told the secret: except by a woman, death does not come to Mahishasura. A female form endowed with every amsha arose. The portion came from Lakshmi, from Mahalakshmi; then from Mother Durga; then from Mother Saraswati; and from the devatas who were there, every power came to her. She arose with eighteen arms. Mahishasura learned that this power had arisen to destroy him. Everyone was speaking of her beauty. At once he fell into moha and sent six rakshasas as his envoys. An envoy is ordinarily one person, or two. He sent six. These are yoga secrets, and Vedavyasa has told them with great care."]$d5ens5json$::jsonb,
