insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'a43a623b-6ca3-4798-a601-d299a504d5f9'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d5ens6title$Six Envoys · The Arishadvargas$d5ens6title$,
  $d5ens6json$["The six are symbols of the arishadvargas, the six inner enemies. Their names are striking: Bhashpala, Durmukha, Tamra, Chichchura, Asiloma, and Bidala. Bhashpa means tears. Bhashpala is the desire that produces tears and sorrow. If the want is not fulfilled, sorrow comes. This is kama — the first of kama, krodha, lobha, moha, mada, matsarya: desire, anger, greed, delusion, pride, and jealousy. These are the principal enemies in sadhana. We practise in order to attain enlightenment, and first these have to be faced. Devi Shakti arose for that. She slays all six. The first event of sadhana is their destruction, dissolved back into our own Shakti. Bhashpala is Kama, the one who brings tears.","Durmukha is the second, krodha, anger. The face has to be seen in a mirror. When anger is there, let the face be looked at. Put on, even once, that terrible look, and see what the face has become: distorted, cruel. The one with that distorted, cruel face is Durmukha. It comes when anger comes. The lines of the face change. Anger keeps coming. It definitely comes to everyone.","The third is Tamra, and he belongs to lobha, greed. Tamra means the colour red. It indicates rajoguna, and the infatuation with wealth. I want this. Miserliness, extreme greed — we call such a person a miser. Tamra is lobha, the third of the six. The fourth is Chichchura. Split the name: chit and chura. Chit is knowledge. Chura is the sword that cuts. What cuts away the discriminating knowledge within is Chichchura. That is moha, delusion. Where there is moha, ignorance grows, and attachment grows.","Dhritarashtra is the picture. Duryodhana does wicked things, and the father has no eye of knowledge: my son, my son. Gandhari, like her husband, bound her own eyes. If children go on a wrong track they should be told so, and guided. Moha encouraged them instead. The Kauravas were misguided. Through the parents themselves, wrong parenting happened. Conscious parenting did not. Chichchura is moha. It binds, and the way to knowledge is closed.","Asiloma is mada, ahankara. In the explanation given here, asi is terrifying with a sword, la is seizing, and uma is bringing nature’s wealth under control. It lives as ‘I alone am great,’ and with that feeling we do what we ought not to do. Duryodhana had this. Even Sri Krishna was accessible — a way to go and learn — and he could not learn. With ‘I alone am great’ he lost all of it. This is the fifth.","The sixth is matsarya, and the asura is Bidala, a cat. It makes no sound, comes with a treacherous mind, and drinks the milk while people sleep, without a footfall. Jealousy in us is like that. We do not know it is there, and an enmity forms. Someone more beautiful, a longer braid, greater earnings, another person’s third eye opening sooner, a companion in meditation reaching further — all of it is matsarya, and it stays subtle.","These enemies are nowhere outside. Vedavyasa put them into the embassy for that reason. The Devi Shakti whom Mahishasura desired is not someone else. She is Prakriti, the Mother, the divine power of Sarvam khalvidam Brahma, and through her the Lord’s principle pervades the creation. The mind fell toward her: I must attain that state. These six stand as obstacles. She slays them and dissolves them. When we meditate, one by one they leave."]$d5ens6json$::jsonb,
