insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'e8b92e52-15b8-4176-ab25-72673dfc20d4'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d6ens3title$Mahishasura · The Summit of Meru$d6ens3title$,
  $d6ens3json$["Today, the sixth day, the teaching is Mahishasura Mardini — the Mother who overcomes Mahishasura, the buffalo-formed power. Yesterday we saw how the Mother of the world took that form, and how many divine powers united in her. The powers of the deities came together and arose as one great power. She is shown with eighteen arms, and she slays Mahishasura. All of this is given as a symbol, and the symbol has to be read.","The first thing told of Mahishasura, after he is born, is that he goes to the summit of Mount Meru and performs tapas. He sits there and meditates. That too is a symbol.","The inner enemies, the arishadvargas, the band of six, were spoken of yesterday. He sends six demons as his messengers. He is seized by moha, infatuation. The inner sense of that infatuation is that he is trying to know the Mother, and so he sends an embassy. But every one of these messengers is an inner enemy. Until they are removed, he cannot come near her. The Mother destroys them all. That same Shakti is what removes the six.","Many enemies lie hidden in us, and many blocks. Kama, desire; krodha, anger; lobha, greed; moha, delusion; mada, pride; matsarya, jealousy — all six are present without our knowing. Even when we tell ourselves that everything is fine, they are there. Victory over the inner enemies comes only through this Shakti, through the power that is lying dormant within.","Mahishasura meditating on the summit of Meru is itself a sign. Meru danda, the staff of Meru, is the spine. The summit is the Sahasrara, the crown. The crown is sarvam khalvidam Brahma: all this is Brahman. At the Ajna, the centre between the eyebrows, we come to know Aham Brahmasmi — I am Brahman. At the Sahasrara we come to know that everyone is divine. Not I alone. Everyone. Even those who play the villain are divine. The asuras too are divine. What lives inside is only divinity. There are asuric qualities; that is all. In one small story, and from how many sides, in how many symbolic forms, Vyasa has given this knowledge.","In the Muladhara, the root centre, she is present as mula shakti, the root power, asleep. It is the same power we meet in the Ramayana: Sita herself, it is said, granted Hanuman the eight siddhis and the nine nidhis. That Sita is this root. In the Dwapara age she is Radha. Here she is Sita. Here she is Mahishasura Mardini, the Mother of the world, Tripureshwari, Lalita. All of them are one. There is no difference. As Kundalini Shakti she abides in the Muladhara."]$d6ens3json$::jsonb,
