insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '3c6a3034-7d7c-49e1-a1fd-d8657c5a2888'::uuid,
  d.id,
  7,
  $d6ens7title$Triputi · Waking, Dream, and Turiya$d6ens7title$,
  $d6ens7json$["We also call this Mother, this Shakti, Triputi. Triputi means that she governs three states. The waking of every living being is under her. The dream of every living being is under her. The deep sleep of every living being is under her. All three rest in her charge.","In the waking state we are called Vishva Purusha, the person of the universe. Whatever is in the Brahmanda, the great cosmos, is also in the Pindanda, the small cosmos of this body. What is in the macrocosm is what is in the microcosm. The name Vishva belongs only to the waking state. Enter the dream, and we are Taijasa Purusha, the person made of light. Enter deep sleep, and we are Prajna, the person of wisdom. She presides over all three. That is why she is Tribhuvaneshwari, and why she is Triputi.","The sadhana of Triputi is to cross the three and enter Turiya, the fourth. For a meditator this is made simple. We travel the three states. Waking is familiar. In dream the subtle body is awake, and dream-experiences come: experiences of meditation, astral experiences, a conscious dreaming. All of that belongs to the dream state. When that too is crossed, we enter deep sleep as Prajna. There, nothing is known. We do not know that we are meditating. We do not know how much time has passed. We do not even know whether we are present. Nothing else is known.","Sometimes a meditator crosses all three and enters Turiya. If that absorption happens unconsciously, it is sushupti, deep sleep. If it happens consciously, it is Turiya. There is only that one difference. When deep sleep has become conscious, that same state is called samadhi, and it is called Turiya. If in that condition we are not conscious, it is ordinary deep sleep.","Because she holds the three, the practice carries us beyond them, and we abide in Turiya. There, whatever has to be known becomes known. It is the state that has crossed every limit: time and space, body and mind, all three gunas, all three states. She is Tripurasundari, the beauty of the three cities. Another name of our own Atman is Tripurasundari. She herself is Tribhuvaneshwari. The Mother has many names. Each context, each situation, each experience, drew one name, when a yogi or a rishi saw her in that way. We are not to be confused by the names. It is one Shakti. This is what a meditator has to know."]$d6ens7json$::jsonb,
