insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '3a38a20a-6cab-414a-a40b-b0b8d738f923'::uuid,
  d.id,
  8,
  $d6ens8title$The Sadhaka's Path · Purity, then Awakening$d6ens8title$,
  $d6ens8json$["The first thing meditation brings is an experience of Shakti. A vast, measureless power is invoked in us. After that, the root power — Parashakti as she sleeps, dormant, in the form of Kundalini Mother — is awakened. Many conditions come while the practice is going on.","At the beginning everything is waking: thoughts, and thoughts about the outer world. Then comes what can be called an altered state of consciousness. It is the dream state. Consciousness shifts. Visions arrive — colours, landscapes, things seen and not understood. Sometimes a rishi appears, and there is no knowing why. We are sitting here, and it feels as though we are somewhere else. A company of rishis seems to meet us and to give messages. These are dream experiences. They are experiences of the subtle body. The gross body corresponds to waking. The subtle body creates the dream. Deep sleep is seen from the causal body. The Shakti who holds all three in her charge is Parashakti. In meditation we come to experience her, and she carries us past the whole arrangement into Turiya.","The weapons of this day are for the meditator as well. Certain weapons come as a gift. They are not held in the hand on the outside. They come inwardly, for victory over the inner enemies. We do not attack anyone. We do not strike back. We do not take up an outer war. The war is inside, and there is a great deal in there that has to be faced. The obstacles are like speed-breakers on a road. Or they are like the high jump of school days: one jump, then the bar raised a little, then another jump. Each crossing reveals, at a subtler level, a larger asura within. Mahishasura is a very great one. The Mother destroys him, and she destroys him with the trishula.","Destruction, for the meditator, means absorption. The asuras in us dissolve. They enter laya. In that Shakti, in that consciousness, the inner enemies and the inner blocks merge, one by one, and disappear. That feeling is the inner meaning. Meditation first creates inner purity. Only after purity comes inner perfection. After shuddhi, purity, comes siddhi. After siddhi comes buddhi, awakened intelligence. First the purified one, the shuddha. Then the perfected one, the siddha. Then the awakened one, the buddha. A meditator grows through these states, and the growth happens through Shakti. These nine days have been set apart for this. See how much it matters.","Ahankara, the I-sense, is a very great inner enemy, and subtle ahankara is greater still. Gross pride can be seen from outside. People say it plainly: you are full of ego. Then we notice. Often we do not know it is in us at all. Subtle ahankara is so fine that its presence is unknown, and it too has to be purified. The state to be reached is freedom from ahankara and from mamakara, the sense of mine."]$d6ens8json$::jsonb,
