insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'f2f01ce6-2f5a-40d9-aa2b-2f6424b9fca3'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d7ens6title$Mamakara · Mine, and the Hand in the Pit$d7ens6title$,
  $d7ens6json$["Mamakara is mine, my people, and it travels as a pair with ahankara. For a sadhaka these two are the most dangerous. They are Sumbha and Nisumbha in another dress: pleasure and pain, I and mine. There is mamakara of the body. This body will be destroyed and will one day go. Hang onto it and the sadhana is forgotten. Caring for the body is right. Giving it all twenty-four hours is not. The body is a rented hut. We took it on rent, and we must leave it. How long does one stay in a rented place? We go home, to our own house.","Identity breeds the same thing. I am a great doctor, a great engineer, a great architect, a great IAS officer, a great minister: I, and mine, and how great my work is. Mamakara toward children is why people are said to change after marriage. Before it, the hold is lighter. After the children come, they become the world. My son, my daughter-in-law, my people. However the boy behaves, even if he is doing wrong, the mamakara encourages him. While that remains, enlightenment is not possible. The energy sticks there.","Then husband and wife. My wife, my husband: a clinging, a possessiveness. She must not laugh or speak with anyone. He must be good to no one but me, and must praise only me. Each takes the other's freedom. The two bind each other. This is a great bondage, and it is why many fear marriage. In other births they married and got stuck. Fear of getting stuck is not a reason to refuse marriage. There is a dharma of Nature here. In this body these bonds exist, and they have to be crossed. Marry. Have children. Come out of mamakara.","True love is not the binding of one by the other. It is unconditional. However you are, I accept you: your past, your present, your future. I love you as you are, and I love your freedom. You were born with a purpose, and that purpose is encouraged. Each helps the other fulfil a life, without the clutch of mine. Bonds must not bind.","Kanaka is gold, and many have a madness for it. However much there is, contentment does not come. Keep gold if it is actually needed. Do not heap it up and hang on. It will go. A knower of the Self lets it go if it goes, and welcomes it if it comes. Things come and go. There is also mamakara of relations and friends. We are universal beings, cosmic human beings; the universe is ours, and everyone is our relation. Mamakara separates us from that. All countries, all planets, the many universes: everyone is our own. Clinging to past memory is mamakara too, whether the memory is of honour or of disgrace.","Opinions are held the same way. Only what I say is correct, and everyone else is wrong. But each person sees with his own sight, if not with insight then with his own angle, and his opinions are his. To be tied to one's own view, and to call the other wrong by reflex, is mamakara. So is the hold on pleasures and enjoyments.","A man fell into a pit and struggled, shouting for help. Someone came and said, give me your hand. He would not. All his life the mamakara of holding money had been strong. He knew how to take. Giving was not in his language. Why should I give my hand? To give, he felt, is to lose something. Another man arrived and changed the sentence. Do not say give. Say take. Take my hand. Then the hand came up. Taking he could do. Giving he could not. This is how I and mine bind us and make us prisoners."]$d7ens6json$::jsonb,
