insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '9bcc0195-2806-4ebc-aadc-195c2c448adc'::uuid,
  d.id,
  7,
  $d7ens7title$Kaushiki · The Drop That Becomes a Thousand$d7ens7title$,
  $d7ens7json$["In these three days we also have to see how Mahasaraswati emerged, because Chanda, Munda, Sumbha, Nisumbha, Dhumralochana, and Raktabija were destroyed by her. From the Devi who had arisen with all the powers, one form came out. Her name is Kaushiki. Kaushiki is Mahasaraswati, the form of knowledge: many weapons, eight arms, and she began to destroy them one by one. For each inner enemy the power puts on a form. Here the form to put on is knowledge. I and mine are cut. The dualities of pleasure and pain, in which we are crushed, are cut. Then there is Raktabija.","Desires in us are not one. After one, ten more come. They do not end, and a human being has no contentment. The other day we called it a good formula: contentment on the material plane, discontent on the spiritual one. Outwardly, be content with what is there. Inwardly, always feel that there is more to learn, and go faster. Raktabija is this multiplication. One desire, one drop of blood, becomes a thousand. Life is spent on the material world alone. When do we realise, and obtain the true education, atma-vidya, brahma-vidya?","Kaushiki took many forms. As Chamundeshwari, as Chandi, she first destroyed Chanda and Munda: the nature that gives pain to everyone, deceit, and the impulse to ruin. She destroyed Dhumralochana, maya. Mind itself is maya. Thoughts are maya, and they keep building a world of it. The defects of the asura rise from tamas and from rajas, from the three gunas. Sattva does not overflow in the same way. But the defect of sattva, when it appears, becomes a subtle ego, and that is especially dangerous.","When Raktabija is to be destroyed she is Kalika. Different names, one Mother. She spread her tongue over the whole earth, so that not a drop of his blood could fall and replicate him in hundreds and thousands. With both hands she took the sword, the sword of knowledge, and she drank the blood. There is a yoga kriya of this moment. It is called Simha Kriya, or Simhasana Kriya. Many do it seated in vajrasana, the kneeling posture. The tongue is put out, large; the eyes are opened wide; the gaze is set at the centre, at the midpoint between the brows. Breath is let out, and the place is toned. Kali drank every desire. On the material plane desire has no end. Right desires may be satisfied. The trouble is the sequence: one crore earned, and a hundred crores wanted; a hundred, and then thousands. Fulfilled, we rejoice. Refused, we suffer, and we are ground between the two. One small desire is enough to bring us back through birth after birth."]$d7ens7json$::jsonb,
