insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '96dd93b6-df7f-4d7b-a27c-3408b4c10432'::uuid,
  d.id,
  8,
  $d7ens8title$What to Ask · Bondage, Buddha, and Janaka$d7ens8title$,
  $d7ens8json$["So a life can be comfortable and still content. What is needed is a small hut, not a mansion. For sadhana, a good hut in the midst of Nature is enough. Ten houses do not add contentment. We do not live in all of them at once. One apartment here and another there: a human being is not filled by the number.","Done fully in vajrasana, Simha Kriya bears on the thyroid, and the thyroid on the vishuddha chakra. The power there clears the blocks, so that what the place creates can heal. Kali's form is fierce, and it frightens enemies. Toward sadhakas there is kindness. She gives whatever is asked: the enjoyments, and moksha as well. She is the giver of liberation. That Mother is the great power in us. Whatever we ask, she gives. The clarity we have to acquire is what to ask. From today we tune to Kaushiki Mahasaraswati. She is Self-knowledge, Brahma-knowledge. That is the direction.","What stops us are the tendencies that bind. Some bonds are all right, and some are kept on purpose. T. Lobsang Rampa, a great yogi, said he carried ninety-nine illnesses. He needed them. Hypertension, a cardiovascular disease: they gave him the feeling of having a body. They anchored him. Without that anchor, he said, he would not stay; he would leave. Some sadhakas and some knowers keep a bondage for that reason. Otherwise the body is simply left.","So look at what is binding you, and why it is still kept. If the sword of knowledge can remove it, remove it. Moksha is freedom, complete freedom. Nothing should bind. In this very moment, be ready to drop anything. With that readiness, enlightenment is in this moment. The Buddha left a kingdom as a blade of grass, in one second. He might have been an emperor. Wealth, all of it, he dropped as if it were grass. No ego, no mamakara, no greatness. No bond held him. He cut them one by one and went to meditate, and he taught the same.","Our homework, these three days, is to see what binds us and to come out of it, whatever it is. Through these nine days the power is ready to give what we ask. There are further stories. Tomorrow we will take King Suratha and the merchant Samadhi. The names already say a great deal. The Mother gave each what he asked. Samadhi, whose name is meditation, asked for Brahma-knowledge and received it. Suratha asked to be a great king, with sovereignty and royal enjoyment, and received exactly that.","A saying carries the same point. One who holds 'I am free' is free; one who holds 'I am bound' is bound. The line is: yā matiḥ sā gatir bhavet. As the mati is, so the gati becomes — as the conviction is, so the course. Ask for bondage and bondage comes. Ask for a kingdom, a post, money, and the life goes that way. Ask for moksha, for Self-knowledge, and she gives that. Ask for both, and she gives both, but then discernment is required, to hold the two in balance. We can live as King Janaka lived. Without Self-knowledge the balance cannot be kept, nor equanimity, nor samata, the even mind and the equal eye."]$d7ens8json$::jsonb,
