insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'caf5a988-71ca-4a1d-a3bf-c6f29a7a2c5b'::uuid,
  d.id,
  9,
  $d7ens9title$The Unripe Fruit · Avadhutas$d7ens9title$,
  $d7ens9json$["She destroyed each of them, and they are our inner enemies. There is much to know about ourselves, including the mask we wear even on this path. Do not think, I am a great meditator. Do the sadhana. The stories have one structure: enemies, a divine power that ends them, and at the last a celebration. The sadhana of the jiva, the obstacles, the victory: the essence does not change. A meditator also has to conquer the inner enemies, and not ignore them, and not wear the badge of 'I meditate.' The action is great. The mask is not.","We have spoken of the arishadvargas, the band of six inner enemies. Right wisdom is needed, and right knowledge. Two things run together. Meditation goes on, and inner purification goes on beside it, and the person moves. Both are required. At the beginning a sadhaka is unripe fruit: a raw mango, a sitaphalam, a custard apple, still raw. Only the ripe fruit is sweet. The raw one is bitter. As purification happens and the inner forces are met with the help of Shakti, the raw fruit ripens. That ripening is enlightenment, maturity in it. Knowledge sprouts, becomes a tender fruit, and must become ripe. The Devi Bhagavatam says these subtleties by way of story. These three days are that ripening.","Fruit now is often ripened with carbide and chemicals, in a hurry. Nothing nourishing remains. It has to be organic, ripened in its own time. A raw mango cannot be ordered to become fruit in two days. There is no crash course that delivers enlightenment in forty days. If there were, the world would long ago have been full of knowers. This is a journey of many births. There is no shortcut.","We already know what state we are in, and we know our inner enemies. Every meditator can feel the blind areas, the darkness inside that is walked around and not faced. This Navaratri is for facing what we have been avoiding. The Mother purifies it. She ends the inner enemy. The raw fruit has to become ripe.","People call a serious meditator mad. Near Nellore there was Venkayya Swami, whom they called Erri Swami, the mad swami. No one understood his speech. Everyone else was mad for money, or for something else. He was mad for God, and only God. I have seen many avadhutas, masters who have let the outer signs fall away. They sit at the side of the road, and from close up we take them for beggars. The world calls them mad. The world is what is mad.","A fellow sadhaka, Chandrashekhar Sharma, used to come to meditate in that region in 1992. He was a good friend, and he practised so deeply that he came to an avadhuta's clarity. Sit in front of him and he would tell past, present, and future; it was crystal clear to him. In Hyderabad he lived on the road, covered with gunny sacks, day and night, through the heat of summer, and the heat did nothing to him. People called him mad.","Once, in 1991 or 1992, he was cooking. The oil boiled over onto his hand. Boiling oil is a severe pain. At that moment someone knocked, wanting to learn meditation. He forgot the burn, sat them down, and taught for two hours. While they sat, the swelling went down and the burned body healed. There was no pain. Ordinarily that skin turns black. It had not. A great master. Many meditators look mad because they have dropped the other desires and kept one: Self-knowledge, Brahma-knowledge. In these Navaratri days the common asking is money, property, this and that. That asking is not wrong. Ask, along with it, for the knowledge of moksha. The Mother is the giver of liberation. She gives all of it."]$d7ens9json$::jsonb,
