insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '09b4c81c-2e99-4ef8-a685-dc6b0a7fe9bb'::uuid,
  d.id,
  11,
  $d7ens11title$Questions · Likeness, and Nachiketa$d7ens11title$,
  $d7ens11json$["Sai Sudha had raised her hand the day before. Her question: from the moment of birth we are not received as ourselves. You are like your father, like your mother. Why? On the physical plane, in some families, comparison is the first response. They match the child to someone. They look at the form, and at the colour: their colour has come. The comparison itself can become a block.","A block forming is not the mistake. The mistake is to go on living inside it after a certain age, after ordinary understanding, after this path has been found. The responsibility is ours. The power to come out of the block is in us, and so is the knowledge. Blocks come through other people to everyone, because this is the physical dimension. We imagine the world as a rose petal, all fragrance. Life is difficult. It is a path of thorns, and we walk on them. The sooner that is known, the sooner a spiritual level and a level of knowledge make it so that nothing here hurts in the old way. We fear death. There is no death for the atma. Whatever happens, the body is what is left. With that seeing, the blocks go. Parents are not all enlightened, and not all wise, and so they compare. Removing what that comparison became is our work. It is inner purification. We ourselves create our ways, and our karmas. Meditation is how we come to know it.","Mahati then asked about Nachiketa in the Kathopanishad. He went to the heavenly world by astral travel, and for three days he was with Yama without hunger or thirst. How can they be without hunger and thirst? And can we, travelling in that same subtle way, go there? When the fire of knowledge is at work, there is the Vishnu granthi, the knot named for Vishnu. If that knot is pierced, can we go?","What Nachiketa gave the world is a great teaching, the principle of the immortal. The atma does not die and cannot be destroyed. He made it known from his own experience, very young, by going to another dimension. Subtle worlds exist. Yamaloka, the world of Yama, is one of them. He waited there, met Yama, spoke with him, and returned. That is not a small thing for a boy. The power and the vibration were in him, so Yama came and spoke. Yama first tried to turn him aside. Ask for something else. Whatever you want, I will give. A thousand years of life. Money. A kingdom. Everything. Nachiketa said that was nothing. He wanted the answers, and he held on. What are these worlds? How do they remain without hunger and thirst? He found it out. The knowledge that actually has to be known is there.","We treat the gross world as the only world. There is a subtle world, a causal world, and beyond them the mahakarana lokas, the great-causal worlds. He grasped that knowledge finely. A meditator can do what he did, and become a Nachiketa: astral travel, the journey of the subtle body. Scientists say these things are not there, because they look from their own angle. A microscope or a telescope cannot bind the atma. No one can produce a photograph of it, or an instrument that measures it. Science does not reach this, and the subtle body is not known there, because the research is research of the gross. A meditator goes into the subtle and learns a great deal: life after death, and life between lives, and what is done before another birth is taken. Those experiences come. What Nachiketa obtained is available. The story has inner meaning. Nachiketa's fire burns ignorance. The Kathopanishad has to be taken with that meaning. Any one Upanishad, held rightly, brings us to Brahma-knowledge. It was already past 7:15, and one more question was taken, from someone who had not spoken in these last days."]$d7ens11json$::jsonb,
  6
