insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '70e993e5-cbd4-4262-ae84-b917d2b9572a'::uuid,
  d.id,
  4,
  $d8ens4title$Time · Experiences · Liberation$d8ens4title$,
  $d8ens4json$["Time is not linear in the way we usually think. Experiences keep repeating until the lesson is learned. Every trigger that appears in life is an opportunity.","The first time we hear about meditation or Self-knowledge, a desire arises within us — ‘I too must attain this Brahma-jnana.’ That desire itself is the beginning of the journey.","Liberation (moksha) is not somewhere else. It is the recognition that what we are seeking is already the nature of the Self. Until that recognition happens, experiences continue to appear as teachers.","Many people feel that spiritual life and worldly life are separate. But every experience in the world is material for the sadhana. Nothing is wasted.","When the same situation repeats, it is not punishment. It is the opportunity to respond differently. The response is what changes the pattern.","In the higher understanding, there is no separate time in which liberation happens. The moment the identification with the limited self drops, what remains is already free.","Until then, the practices — meditation, self-inquiry, Devi Samarpan — prepare the ground. They reduce the density of identification.","Experiences of joy and sorrow both have a purpose. They show us where we are still holding on. When the holding loosens, the experience no longer binds.","The seeker often asks, ‘When will I be free?’ The more accurate question is, ‘What is still being held as mine?’ The answer to that question is the next step.","Time appears long only when we are waiting for something outside. When the attention turns inward, the sense of waiting reduces.","Every day of Navaratri is such a turning. The outer festival ends, but the inner turning can continue.","Liberation is not the destruction of the world. It is the end of the mistaken belief that the world is separate from the Self.","Until that end happens, we continue the practices with patience. Patience itself is a form of surrender.","The stories and teachings we hear are not for entertainment. They are mirrors. Whatever touches us is pointing to something within.","Today, notice one repeating pattern in your life. Instead of resisting it, offer it in the evening Samarpan. See what happens.","This is practical spirituality. It does not require special conditions. It requires only honesty and willingness.","As the days of Navaratri progress, the cumulative effect of the practices becomes more visible. Small shifts compound.","Let us continue with that understanding."]$d8ens4json$::jsonb,
