insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '14b1d74f-1e02-42a2-aa61-af83f71741b1'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d8ens6title$King Suratha · The Story of Samadhi$d8ens6title$,
  $d8ens6json$["The story of King Suratha from the Devi Mahatmyam is relevant here. After losing his kingdom, he went to the forest and met the merchant Samadhi. Both were suffering from attachment.","Through the guidance of the sage Medhas, they performed intense sadhana and ultimately had the vision of the Mother. The story shows that even those who are deeply entangled in worldly life can attain the vision of Devi when they turn toward Her with sincerity.","King Suratha represents the mind that has lost its outer supports and is forced to look within. Samadhi represents the one who has already begun to turn inward.","Their meeting is the meeting of two seekers at different stages. Both are accepted by the Mother.","The sage Medhas does not give them complicated practices. He points them to the worship of the Mother and to self-inquiry. Simplicity is the mark of true guidance.","When the vision of Devi arises, the earlier losses no longer appear as losses. They appear as the necessary conditions for the vision.","This is the secret of the story: what the world calls failure can be the doorway. The Mother uses every situation.","In our own lives, the moments when outer supports fall away are often the moments when the inner support becomes visible — if we turn toward Her.","The story also shows that sadhana done with others has its own power. Suratha and Samadhi supported each other’s practice.","Today, if any outer situation feels like a loss, offer it to the Mother with the same sincerity. See whether it begins to reveal a deeper purpose.","The Devi Mahatmyam is not ancient history. It is a map of the inner journey that repeats in every sincere seeker.","Let us take the essence of the story into today’s practice."]$d8ens6json$::jsonb,
