insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '7667a19e-0f85-42a9-aea7-192416a01416'::uuid,
  d.id,
  7,
  $d8ens7title$The Seven Planes of Knowledge (Sapta Jnana Bhumikas)$d8ens7title$,
  $d8ens7json$["This is one of the most important sections of today’s talk. The seven planes of knowledge (Sapta Jnana Bhumikas) give a clear map for the sadhaka.","The first plane is Shubheccha — the arising of a pure desire for liberation. Without this desire, the journey does not begin. It is a gift.","The second plane is Vicharana — inquiry. The mind begins to ask, ‘Who am I? What is the nature of this world?’ This inquiry is sustained by the earlier desire.","The third plane is Tanumanasa — the attenuation of the mind. Gross identifications weaken. The mind becomes thinner, less reactive.","The fourth plane is Sattvapatti — the predominance of sattva. The light of the Self begins to shine through. Peace and clarity increase.","The fifth plane is Asamsakti — natural non-attachment. Things are seen, but they no longer bind in the same way.","The sixth plane is Padartha-bhavana — the recognition that the world has no independent reality apart from the Self. The sense of separation thins further.","The seventh plane is Turiya — the state beyond waking, dream, and deep sleep. Here the notions of karma, rebirth, and doership do not arise.","Until one reaches the higher planes, karma and the sense of individuality continue to operate. The map helps the sadhaka understand where he or she stands.","These planes are not rigid stages that one climbs like a ladder in a fixed time. They are descriptions of shifts in understanding and identification.","A person may touch a higher plane momentarily and then return. The practice is to stabilise the higher seeing.","The first three planes are more accessible through effort and grace. The later planes are more the result of grace, though effort prepares the ground.","Many seekers remain in the early planes for a long time. There is no fault in this. Each plane has its own beauty and lessons.","The danger is to claim a higher plane prematurely. Honesty about one’s actual state is itself a sign of progress.","In the Turiya state, the questions of karma and rebirth do not arise because the one who would be bound by them is not found. This is not a theory; it is the report of those who have stabilised there.","For those of us who have not reached that plane, the practices remain essential. They are the means by which the density of identification is reduced.","The Sapta Jnana Bhumikas also help in understanding the experiences that arise in meditation. Some experiences belong to the early planes; some indicate a shift.","When sattva increases, the mind naturally becomes more meditative. Forcing meditation when rajas or tamas dominate is less effective than first cultivating sattva.","Asamsakti does not mean running away from responsibilities. It means performing them without the earlier heavy sense of ‘I am the doer and the enjoyer.’","In the sixth plane, the world continues to appear, but it is known as non-separate. This is different from denying the world.","The seventh plane is not a blank state. It is the natural state in which the three ordinary states are seen as appearances within awareness.","Dr. Newton presents these planes so that sadhakas have a clear reference. Without a map, one can mistake temporary experiences for final realisation.","The map also prevents discouragement. If one knows that the early planes involve struggle, the struggle is not taken as failure.","Grace operates at every plane. The desire for liberation itself is grace. The appearance of a teacher is grace. The capacity to practise is grace.","Today, reflect quietly: which plane feels closest to your current understanding? There is no need to announce it. Just see honestly.","The purpose of the map is not to create a new identity (‘I am on the fourth plane’). The purpose is to support the journey.","As we continue the Navaratri sadhana, these planes can be remembered. Each day’s practice contributes to the movement through them.","In the evening, when you do Devi Samarpan, you can offer even the desire to progress through the planes. The Mother knows the right pace.","The seven planes ultimately point beyond themselves. The highest teaching is that the Self is already free, and the planes describe the falling away of what was never real.","Until that falling away is complete, the planes remain a compassionate guide.","Let us keep this map in the background as we practise.","Many questions about rebirth, karma, and liberation are answered differently depending on the plane from which one speaks. This resolves many apparent contradictions in the scriptures.","From the early planes, karma is real and must be worked through. From the seventh plane, karma is not found. Both statements are true in their context.","The sadhaka’s task is to move from the context in which bondage is real to the context in which it is not. The practices are the bridge.","Patience is required because the movement is not always linear. There can be periods of apparent stagnation that are actually integration.","When integration happens, the next shift occurs more naturally. Forcing creates tension that slows the process.","The seventh plane is sometimes called the natural state because nothing new is added. Only the superimposed limitations are no longer believed.","In practical terms, this means that the problems which seemed central begin to lose their centrality. They may still appear, but they no longer define the person.","This is the fruit that the Navaratri sadhana is oriented toward — not a change in outer circumstances alone, but a change in the sense of who is experiencing them.","The Sapta Jnana Bhumikas give language to that change so that it can be recognised and stabilised.","Today’s teaching is an invitation to use this language gently, without turning it into another concept to hold.","Offer the entire map, and the desire to understand it, to the Mother. Let Her guide the actual movement.","This completes the main exposition of the seven planes.","We will now move to the practice of Devi Samarpan, which is the living application of everything that has been said.","Remember: the planes are descriptions. The practice is the reality.","With this clarity, let us enter the practice."]$d8ens7json$::jsonb,
  14
