insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '60fee787-d910-4ac0-a90b-a5d2eadf9d62'::uuid,
  d.id,
  2,
  $d10ens2title$Navaratri Practice and Inner Energy$d10ens2title$,
  $d10ens2json$["Energy moves in these two directions. During the nine days of Navaratri, meditators remain more inwardly focused rather than outwardly engaged so that they may experience energy directly. Vijayadashami marks the beginning of victory: after storing energy within like a reservoir, we begin applying it in the outer world. We can observe both directions of energy. Within, energy can move upward or downward. The fourth point we discussed is that energy expressed outwardly helps us accomplish our work.","Whatever task we intend to complete, energy can help us bring it to fulfillment. The Pandavas, after their period of living incognito, retrieved the weapons they had hidden near the Shami tree, and from Vijayadashami onward they achieved victories they had not achieved before. There is a deeper meaning here. Energy activated through spiritual practice serves two purposes: accomplishment of work and attainment of knowledge. When it moves inward and upward, it leads to Self-knowledge and knowledge of Brahman. In the outer, material world, events, situations, and difficulties constantly arise, and we search for solutions.","We ask: Why is this happening? Why did this situation arise? When will this unbearable condition end? Why am I suffering? The root causes of external events must be sought within. What happens in the gross world also occurs in the subtle world. We must go still deeper, to the causal world, to discover the causes. This is why we speak of the causal body. It contains the design, plan, or pattern underlying events. If we keep searching only outside for the causes of what happens outside, we will never find them.","Everything has a relationship between cause and effect. According to this spiritual understanding, the causes of events in the outer world exist in the causal body and causal world. Only through meditation can we enter the great causal state and then go beyond causes, understanding them clearly. When both cause and effect are understood, a solution appears and the issue can be resolved. Albert Einstein is said to have observed that a problem cannot be solved at the same level of mind that created it. We must transcend the mind to find the solution. The Devi Mahatmyam offers a symbolic account of this. Lord Vishnu rests in yogic sleep.","The story says that Madhu and Kaitabha, two demons, arose from the wax of Vishnu's ears. These are symbols. Without understanding their inner meaning, we might laugh at the idea of demons emerging from earwax. Vishnu fought the two demons, who kept disturbing him, for five thousand years without defeating them. Five thousand years symbolically means a very long struggle. When he sought the help of Mahamaya, the Divine Mother and her Shakti, the demons were destroyed. The meaning is that we cannot conquer the mind by fighting it. Thinking is the mind's function. If we try to stop thoughts forcibly and declare war on the mind, we are the ones who lose.","The lesson of Vishnu's story is that we must go beyond the mind and draw upon divine energy rather than fight the mind. By awakening Shakti, we can rise to a state beyond it. Puranic stories contain profound meanings and knowledge; we should remember this whenever we read them. Events in the gross world have corresponding subtle patterns. The energetic or astral template, its design, and its root causes lie in the causal world and causal body.","Through meditation and the help of Shakti, we can reach the great causal state and go beyond cause and effect. We often discuss waking, dreaming, and deep sleep. We must transcend these three states and enter turiya. In that state, the bonds of cause and effect begin to fall away."]$d10ens2json$::jsonb,
