insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  '84794234-6b7f-4023-abc5-503584ed0913'::uuid,
  d.id,
  3,
  $d10ens3title$Spiritual Stories and Their Inner Meanings$d10ens3title$,
  $d10ens3json$["The sixth point concerns tamoguna and rajoguna. These qualities can obstruct the flow of energy and influence much of what happens within us. They should not dominate us. When tamas and rajas are excessive, we behave like the asuras. Sattva should predominate, leading us toward a pure sattvic state. Who has more energy? Those who meditate regularly and continuously. Their sattva increases. Laziness and negativity diminish; ego, possessiveness, attachment, and aversion gradually disappear. This is the inner battle against tamas and rajas symbolized by the slaying of Mahishasura during Navaratri.","These qualities act as blocks to energy. When energy flows strongly, it clears obstructions and redirects a flow that has gone astray. Consider a pipe blocked inside: a strong gush of water can clear the blockage. Similarly, we carry emotional and memory blocks—old fears, anger, sorrow, and many other emotions hardened within us. Intense practice, especially during favorable periods such as Navaratri, gradually clears them. This is the seventh point: purification of the network of nadis, the subtle energy channels. Obstacles are removed and the flow of energy finds a clear direction.","Vijayadashami has many inner meanings. We may also understand it as victory over the ten senses. When Shakti is awakened within, we gain greater control over our senses.","In North India, people burn an effigy of Ravana on Vijayadashami. Ravana's ten heads being destroyed symbolically reminds us to dissolve the ten-headed ego within ourselves. Ravana represents rajoguna and a level of development associated with the Manipura chakra. The battle between Rama and Ravana also carries an inner meaning. Some scriptures describe Rama himself undertaking spiritual practice before this great battle, because a great mission requires great energy and energy requires practice. Though Rama is understood as an incarnation of Vishnu, he lived in a human body and went through the human process of learning. He learned from Vishvamitra, who later directed him to the higher teacher Vasishta. Rama asked Vasishta many questions, expressing human emotions and sorrow, even asking why suffering exists and saying that he felt deeply depressed. Vasishta answered patiently. Their remarkable spiritual discussions are presented in thousands of verses. Before the war with Ravana, Rama too engaged in practice.","Great energy is needed to win a war. A great battle is also taking place within our minds, and we need energy to win it. These stories speak symbolically about that inner struggle. During Navaratri, Mahishasura's defeat represents victory over tamoguna. We should also remember the extraordinary episode of the Pandavas: they spent twelve years in the forest and one year living incognito. Why? We can understand that period too as a form of spiritual practice. The twelve years in the forest represent long, dedicated sadhana."]$d10ens3json$::jsonb,
  4
from public.days d
