insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'a4049735-877e-41de-af72-f9c95cdc9c3c'::uuid,
  d.id,
  4,
  $d10ens4title$The Importance of Practice in Life$d10ens4title$,
  $d10ens4json$["Then came one year of ajnatavasa, living incognito. There is a profound meaning here. Forest life symbolizes silence, quietness, and meditative practice undertaken for a purpose. After twelve years of that practice, the Pandavas, although royal family members, had to live for a year without revealing their royal identity. They adopted disguises and ordinary roles.","Yudhishthira, also called Dharmaraja, became Kankabhatta, a companion and adviser in King Virata's court who played dice with the king. Bhima became the cook Vallabha. Arjuna became Brihannala, a dance teacher to Uttara, concealing his identity. Nakula cared for horses under another name.","Sahadeva, as Tantripala, tended cattle. Draupadi became Sairandhri, an attendant to Queen Sudeshna. Why did they do this? After twelve years of sadhana, subtle forms of ego can arise: “I have gained powers; I have acquired siddhis; whatever I wish happens.” Such ego becomes the first enemy of the spiritual seeker. Many practitioners become attached to extraordinary powers. The Pandavas' year of anonymity symbolizes conquering that ego.","During this period they did not misuse or display their strength. They kept it inwardly contained and concealed their weapons in a Shami or Jammi tree: Arjuna's Gandiva bow, Bhima's mace, arrows, and their other weapons. Weapons symbolize powers. A meditator should not display powers to impress others. Energy is not for personal glory; it is to be used at the right time for a great purpose. This is the deeper significance of honoring the Shami tree in India, a meaning we should not forget. Likewise, however great our outward identity may be—doctor, engineer, scientist, person of wealth or high position—we should set that identity aside. Bhima was not presenting himself as Bhima; he was a cook. Even one day of living without our usual identity could teach us something. We may not be able to live incognito for a whole year, but we can try setting aside the many masks and burdens of “I am this” or “I am that.” In the past, kings sometimes traveled in disguise, stayed among ordinary people, ate with them, and learned how they truly lived, because people often praised the king to his face and concealed their complaints. For meditators, the real lesson of ajnatavasa is to withdraw identification from the physical and social personality and remain grounded in spiritual identity. Indian festivals and epics carry extraordinary insights. While the Pandavas were at Virata's court, they faced many tests. Kichaka repeatedly humiliated Draupadi. Bhima and Dharmaraja felt anger, but they could not reveal their identities. Bhima could have killed him in a moment with one blow, yet they had to wait for the right time."]$d10ens4json$::jsonb,
