insert into public.sections (id, day_id, section_order, title, content, minutes)
select
  'd7d84e43-65ec-41f6-ad5e-0bd625e0ad9c'::uuid,
  d.id,
  6,
  $d10ens6title$Inner Transformation and Principles of Success$d10ens6title$,
  $d10ens6json$["People undertake many tasks, but at the very beginning they think, “Perhaps I cannot do this; perhaps I do not have enough energy.” Once that feeling of inferiority is planted like a seed, it blocks the expression of their energy. We should begin every project with a noble and confident attitude. Second, victory is not about conquering the outer world but conquering the inner world. Along with our ten senses, the mind creates many obstacles: emotions, narrow thoughts, and ego. These are the real blocks.","During Navaratri, we symbolically destroy the inner Mahishasura and the other demons—Shumbha, Nishumbha, Dhumralochana, Raktabija, Madhu, Kaitabha, Vritrasura, and others. They are within us as negative qualities. How can we succeed in any project while these inner enemies dominate? Mahishasura symbolizes tamoguna, which expresses itself in many ways. Third, many people keep saying they will act in the future. But that imagined future never arrives. Every action and victory happens only in the present moment. We must succeed here and now. Leave the past behind on the riverbank. If we keep carrying old wounds, we cannot attain victory.","Anyone seeking genuine success must first win inwardly. When you win within, you can succeed outwardly and inspire many others to succeed. The nine days of Navaratri are dedicated to inner victory. Every spiritual discipline is a practice, and practice takes effort. A musician does not become accomplished in a single day; behind one beautiful song lie countless hours of practice. The same is true of an instrument, dance, sculpture, and meditation. Meditation requires sadhana.","Practice matters both inwardly and outwardly. Some say meditation needs no practice, that one can simply sit and meditate. But the mind pulls us in many directions. First we must train the body to sit and the eyes to remain closed. Eventually meditation becomes effortless, but until then practice is needed. Some observe Ayudha Puja on Navami and others on Vijayadashami. What does it mean? We thank the instruments and objects that help us—our vehicles, cars, bikes, and tools. We often take them to a temple and perform rituals, but we should understand their inner purpose.","We are expressing gratitude and tuning ourselves to these instruments, recognizing them as divine instruments useful in achieving our goals. Our greatest instruments are our body and mind. We must care for them and thank the body. We must use energy well, for accomplishment and for Atma Jnana and Brahma Jnana. Whatever we learn through meditation and practice, we should share with everyone. We seek Self-knowledge inwardly and teach what we have learned outwardly. Victory also rests on the principle of Karma Yoga: dedicated action and effort. On the physical plane, effort is necessary because there is a strong gravitational pull.","Next is the principle of gratitude. We must first thank and bow to our mother, because our first victory began with her. Behind our mother we honor Jagannmata, the Divine Mother who is the source of our energy. Then comes the principle of Aparajita, the undefeated one. Before retrieving their weapons from the Shami tree, the Pandavas connected with Goddess Aparajita and then took up their weapons."]$d10ens6json$::jsonb,
  4
